<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Southern Han</h1><p class="st-meta">917 CE – 971 CE · capital Xingwang (Guangzhou)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A kingdom ruled from Guangzhou by the Liu family, rich from maritime trade but notorious for its cruelty and its vast corps of eunuchs.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-yin-southern-han">Liu Yin</a>, of a family that had grown wealthy in the southern trade, became military governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingnan">Lingnan</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a> late in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a>. His brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-yan-southern-han">Liu Yan</a> inherited the post and in 917 proclaimed himself emperor of Great Yue, renaming the state Han in 918, with the capital renamed Xingwang.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Southern Han controlled modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxi">Guangxi</a> and grew rich on the sea trade with South-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>, handled by Arab, Persian and other foreign merchants at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a>. Its rulers were notorious for elaborate tortures and lavish palaces; under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-chang">Liu Chang</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a> numbered in the thousands and dominated government. In 938 its fleet was destroyed by Ngô Quyền at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bach-dang-river">Bạch Đằng</a> river, ending Chinese rule in northern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Vietnam</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 971 a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a> army under Pan Mei invaded; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-chang">Liu Chang</a> burned his treasures and surrendered. He lived on at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> as a pensioned noble, and the far south was reunited with the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/southern-han.jpg" alt="Southern Han" width="320" height="309"><figcaption>Image: Ian Kiu · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Five_Dynasties_Ten_Kingdoms_923_CE.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>917 CE – 971 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Xingwang (Guangzhou)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Liu Yan (Liu Gong), Liu Sheng, Liu Chang</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mahayana Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese, Cantonese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a>, <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Great Yue, Nan Han</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=944&amp;focus=southern-han" aria-label="Southern Han on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7705 1324 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Southern Han lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8025 1580L7901 1174"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7901 1174h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8025 1580h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8025 1580h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=944&amp;focus=southern-han">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

